{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:16530519 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:16530519 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"16530519","source":"MED","pmid":"16530519","doi":"10.1053/j.gastro.2005.12.031","title":"Fatty acid ethyl esters cause pancreatic calcium toxicity via inositol trisphosphate receptors and loss of ATP synthesis.","authorString":"Criddle DN, Murphy J, Fistetto G, Barrow S, Tepikin AV, Neoptolemos JP, Sutton R, Petersen OH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Criddle DN","firstName":"David N","lastName":"Criddle","initials":"DN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2952-8450"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Research Council Secretory Control Research Group, Physiological Laboratory, University of Liverpool, Liverpool, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Murphy J","firstName":"John","lastName":"Murphy","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Fistetto G","firstName":"Gregorio","lastName":"Fistetto","initials":"G"},{"fullName":"Barrow S","firstName":"Stephanie","lastName":"Barrow","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Tepikin AV","firstName":"Alexei V","lastName":"Tepikin","initials":"AV","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8172-7513"}},{"fullName":"Neoptolemos JP","firstName":"John P","lastName":"Neoptolemos","initials":"JP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6201-7399"}},{"fullName":"Sutton R","firstName":"Robert","lastName":"Sutton","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Petersen OH","firstName":"Ole H","lastName":"Petersen","initials":"OH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6998-0380"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6201-7399"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6998-0380"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8172-7513"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2952-8450"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"130","journalIssueId":1302315,"dateOfPublication":"2006 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2006,"printPublicationDate":"2006-03-01","journal":{"title":"Gastroenterology","medlineAbbreviation":"Gastroenterology","isoabbreviation":"Gastroenterology","nlmid":"0374630","essn":"1528-0012","issn":"0016-5085"}},"pubYear":"2006","pageInfo":"781-793","abstractText":"<h4>Background & aims</h4>Fatty acid ethyl esters are ethanol metabolites inducing sustained, toxic elevations of the acinar cytosolic free calcium ion concentration ([Ca(2+)](C)) implicated in pancreatitis. We sought to define the mechanisms of this elevation.<h4>Methods</h4>Isolated mouse pancreatic acinar cells were loaded with fluorescent dyes for confocal microscopy to measure [Ca(2+)](C) (Fluo 4, Fura Red), endoplasmic reticulum calcium ion concentration ([Ca(2+)](ER), Mg Fluo 4), mitochondrial membrane potential (TMRM), ADP:ATP ratio (Mg Green), and NADH autofluorescence in response to palmitoleic acid ethyl ester and palmitoleic acid (10-100 micromol/L). Whole-cell patch clamp was used to measure the calcium-activated chloride current and apply ethanol metabolites and/or ATP intracellularly.<h4>Results</h4>Intracellular delivery of ester induced oscillatory increases of [Ca(2+)](C) and calcium-activated currents, inhibited acutely by caffeine (20 mmol/L), but not atropine, indicating involvement of inositol trisphosphate receptor channels. The stronger effect of extracellular ester or acid caused depletion of [Ca(2+)](ER), not prevented by caffeine, but associated with depleted ATP, depleted NADH autofluorescence, and depolarized mitochondria, suggesting calcium-ATPase pump failure because of lack of ATP. Intracellular ATP abolished the sustained rise in [Ca(2+)](C), although oscillatory signals persisted that were prevented by caffeine. Inhibition of ester hydrolysis markedly reduced its calcium-releasing effect and consequent toxicity.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Fatty acid ethyl ester increases [Ca(2+)](C) through inositol trisphosphate receptors and, following hydrolysis, through calcium-ATPase pump failure from impaired mitochondrial ATP production. Lowering cellular fatty acid substrate concentrations may reduce cell injury in pancreatitis.","affiliation":"Medical Research Council Secretory Control Research Group, Physiological Laboratory, University of Liverpool, Liverpool, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"G8801575","agency":"Medical Research Council","acronym":"MRC_","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pancreas","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects","majorTopic_YN":"Y"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Endoplasmic Reticulum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mitochondria","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pancreatitis, Alcoholic","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"PC","qualifierName":"prevention & control","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Calcium","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"Y"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ethanol","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"Y"},{"abbreviation":"TO","qualifierName":"toxicity","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Caff